# UNVERIFIED — pending debrief
# §4.9 names the arguments of a call when two parameters share a type, and the
# rule reads a DECLARATION's names. A function type had none at any of its
# sites, so a call made through a value of one was positional and handed its two
# `str` over the wrong way round in silence (defect 026, panel 128).
#
# The repair is this refusal. A function type whose parameters can be confused
# names them, the names are part of the type, and a call through it is checked
# the way a call to a declaration is. No fix is offered on purpose: the reader
# has to choose two names that say which is which, and a compiler that invented
# `a` and `b` would hand back the shape the rule exists to refuse.
#
# The second diagnostic is not a cascade of the first, which is why it is pinned
# here rather than suppressed: the names are part of the TYPE, so `pair` fits
# this parameter only once the two agree on them. Naming the type `(from, to)`
# would leave that line exactly as red.
function relay(f: (function(str, str) -> str)) -> str  #~ needs_parameter_names
    return f("one", "two")

function main()
    print(relay(pair))  #~ type_mismatch

function pair(code: str, msg: str) -> str
    return code + "/" + msg
